{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And these are the sons of Zibeon: Aiah and Anah</b>. The vav is superfluous, and similarly, \"your servant and I from then and now and I am your servant\" (II Samuel 15:34) — the rule for both is 'I,' and \"these are the heads of the fathers' houses: Epher and Ishi\" (I Chronicles 5:24), and many like these. Now this Zibeon is the third son of Seir the Horite, and he fathered these two sons, Aiah and Anah. Scripture relates that this Anah son of Zibeon is that same Anah who found the yemim in the wilderness while pasturing the donkeys of Zibeon his father, in order to distinguish between him and Anah his uncle, the brother of Zibeon his father, and this Anah son of Zibeon is the father-in-law of Esav." "<b>Who found the yemim</b>. According to the view of some of our Sages in the Talmud (Pesahim 54a), they are mules. This man discovered that they produce offspring from a different species, which is not the case with other hybrid species. And it says, \"in the wilderness while pastering the donkeys,\" because he had there in the wilderness many male donkeys mounting she-donkeys, and he bred them with mares and they gave birth. It appears that he was considered a wise man in his generation, for he recognized from their species that they are close in nature and would breed, and this became known through that event, and therefore Scripture describes him by it. Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 36:24) translated \"gibbaraya\" (mighty ones), and what appears from his reasoning is that this Anah was attacked by the nation called Eimim — formerly a great and lofty people like the Anakim (Deuteronomy 2:10) — and they sought to steal from him the donkeys of Zibeon his father, while he was in the wilderness with none to help him, but he overpowered them (lit. 'his hand found them') and saved them from their hand. This is from the idiom תמצא ידך לכל אויביך (Psalms 21:9), ולא המציתך ביד דוד (II Samuel 3:8). Or one may say that he encountered them and prevailed, and he became known through this act of valor, and this is correct."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {0 "Ramban reads the verse's opening word ואיה as 'Aiah and Anah,' with the initial vav on ואיה treated as superfluous, and he marshals other cases of superfluous vav to support this."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:40:48.946565944Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}